/* Shared, cross-window RCSS for the modern theme: generic (non-positional) rules reused by more
   than one migrated window, plus the anchor/center/stretch layout utility classes every window
   uses. Every migrated window's <head> links this file first, then its own per-window positional
   .rcss.

   Second generation: blackened iron / dark forged metal, replacing the first generation's
   cool-steel/blue-gray direction (see tokens.ini's own header comment). Colors/borders below are
   named against this theme's design-token table -- keep the trailing token-name comment next to
   each value; that table is the source of truth, this file is its first application.

   Depth primitives (.modern-frame/.modern-panel/.modern-inset, .btn/.checkbox-box below) simulate
   raised/recessed physical surfaces using per-side border-color bevels (RmlUi supports
   border-top/right/bottom/left-color as independent properties) plus `decorator:
   linear-gradient(...)`, which IS a real working decorator here (proven by main_frame.rcss's
   gauge fills before this). Light source is fixed upper-left throughout: a RAISED surface gets a
   lighter top/left border and a lighter gradient corner there, darker bottom/right; a RECESSED
   surface inverts both. Never mix the two directions on one element.

   Real `box-shadow` (inset and outset, genuine blur, comma-separated multiple shadows) IS
   supported by this vendored renderer as of the SDL_GPU backend's PR-989 integration -- confirmed
   by reading the actual source, not assumed: PropertyParserBoxShadow.cpp parses the full CSS
   syntax, GeometryBoxShadow.cpp renders it through RenderManager::PushLayer/CompositeLayers/
   filters, and RmlUi_Renderer_SDL_GPU.cpp genuinely implements PushLayer, CompositeLayers,
   CompileFilter and RenderBlur (this is a still-local, not-yet-merged renderer change -- see
   src/ThirdParty/RmlUi's own git history). The login dialog now uses real box-shadow directly for
   shadow/glow effects a border color can't express (window drop-shadow, header/
   hero-plate cast-shadow + inset highlight, body recess); the per-side border-color bevel
   technique above stays the right tool for a raised/recessed SURFACE's own edge. One conversion
   gotcha: this engine's rgba() parses alpha via a 0-255 integer (atoi), NOT a 0-1 CSS float --
   `rgba(0,0,0,.62)` parses as alpha 0 (invisible). Every box-shadow color below is pre-converted
   to the 0-255 form. */

body {
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	margin: 0;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* Full-screen dim overlay for modal-style windows (SysMenuWin/OptionWin). */
#backdrop {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	background-color: token(surface-deep);
	pointer-events: auto;
}

.checkbox-row {
	position: absolute;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
/* Only login.rml consumes this today (it links base.rcss directly). Restrained/small on purpose --
   the reference's checkbox is a small recessed control whose gold indicates checked state rather
   than dominating the form; this used to be a much larger, brighter block that competed with the
   title/inputs for visual weight. Palette matches the reference's own checkbox exactly
   (unchecked border #514a35/background #0c0e0b; checked border #aa8132, gradient #d3aa4e ->
   #8f6922) rather than the theme's general-purpose accent-gold token, which is noticeably
   brighter/more saturated than this specific control's own reference tone -- no decorator/
   per-side bevel on the unchecked state, matching the reference's own flat treatment. */
.checkbox-box {
	display: inline-block;
	width: 12dp;
	height: 12dp;
	background-color: #0c0e0b;
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #514a35;
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	box-shadow: inset 0 1dp 2dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 140);
}
.checkbox-box.checked {
	background-color: #8f6922;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #d3aa4e, #8f6922);
	border-color: #aa8132;
}
.checkbox-label {
	position: absolute;
	left: 18dp; /* 12dp box + reference's own 6px flex gap */
	top: 0;
	white-space: nowrap;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-size: 9dp;
	color: token(text-secondary);
}

/* Flat dark-iron fill with a visible metal-frame border -- steel is the general interactive
   accent (hover/focus), gold is reserved for selected-state/text emphasis, never general button
   chrome. */
/* Per-side border bevel (light top/left, dark bottom/right) is the same "raised surface" language
   .modern-frame already uses -- a single uniform border-color reads as a web button, not a
   forged-metal one.

   Colors/box-shadow/font below match the reference's own `.btn` EXACTLY (verified against both
   the login and game-menu reference stylesheets -- byte-identical between the two beyond
   position/sizing, confirming this really is one universal component, not a per-window look).
   This used to only be applied to login.rcss's own private `.login-btn` copy -- a real bug, not a
   style choice: every OTHER consumer of this shared class (sys_menu's 4 stacked buttons, msg_win/
   remember_password_prompt's OK/Cancel, char_make/char_sel_main/credit_win/mu_helper_bar/
   my_quest_info/server_select) was still stuck on the old un-corrected look. Fixing it here once
   makes every consumer pick up the real reference treatment for free -- see login.rcss's own
   .login-btn, now reduced to position/size only since color is inherited from here. border-radius
   changed from token(radius-md) to token(radius-sm) to match the reference's own `border-radius:
   1px` (this theme's second-generation radius scale, tokens.ini's own "1/2, not 3/4" note). */
.btn {
	position: absolute;
	width: 108dp;
	height: 30dp;
	text-align: center;
	vertical-align: middle;
	line-height: 30dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-size: 9dp;
	letter-spacing: 0.18dp;
	color: #c0b9a4;
	background-color: #13140f;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 255, 255, 10),
		transparent 45%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #26251d, #13140f 58%, #0a0b09);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #4a4738;
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(232, 215, 168, 14),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 #000000,
		0 1dp 2dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 153);
}
.btn:hover {
	color: #e0c476;
	border-color: #776646;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 255, 255, 13),
		transparent 45%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #302b1f, #18160f 58%, #0c0c09);
}
.btn:active {
	/* Reference: `transform:translateY(1px); box-shadow:inset 0 1px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.72)` -- a real
	   1dp downward shift plus one inset shadow, replacing the earlier bevel-inversion technique
	   (background/border-*-color swap) invented before real box-shadow and translate were both
	   confirmed reliable together on this renderer. Native :active pseudo-class, unverified
	   against this branch's own mouse-input routing (RmlUi's default SDL translation is bypassed
	   here for unrelated reasons) -- degrades gracefully if it never fires. */
	transform: translateY(1dp);
	box-shadow: inset 0 1dp 3dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 184);
}
.btn.disabled {
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: #0a0b09;
	color: token(semantic-neutral);
	border-color: rgba(70, 70, 59, 60); /* metal-mid's rgb @ 60 alpha, stays neutral when disabled, no gold */
	opacity: 0.55;
	cursor: default;
}

/* Opt-in primary/hero variant -- apply alongside .btn (class="btn btn-ok") for a
   window's one confirm-style action; plain .btn above stays flat/neutral, unaffected, still the
   right choice for a list of equal-weight actions (sys_menu.rcss's four stacked menu buttons).
   Overrides only color properties, not layout -- .btn's own position/size/border-width apply
   underneath. Must stay textually after .btn/.btn:hover/.btn:active above (equal specificity once
   combined with .btn on the same element, source order decides).

   Values match the reference's own `.btn-primary` exactly (same source as login.rcss's own
   .login-btn-ok, now reduced to position-only): real box-shadow for depth, no border-top-color
   bevel or outline() font-effect -- the reference specifies neither. */
.btn-ok {
	color: #e5c35c;
	background-color: #351009;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 212, 151, 18),
		transparent 38%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #651b12, #351009 58%, #160706);
	border-color: #733022;
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(255, 218, 159, 23),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 #090201,
		0 2dp 5dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 128);
}
.btn-ok:hover {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom,
		rgba(255, 212, 151, 26),
		transparent 38%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #7a2116, #451309 58%, #1c0907);
	border-color: #9a4028;
}
/* No :active override -- the reference gives .btn-primary no separate pressed state either; the
   generic .btn:active above applies uniformly to both variants, matching its actual cascade. */
.btn-ok.disabled {
	/* .btn.disabled's own flat neutral background-color/border-color/opacity already apply
	   (two-class selector, same specificity as this rule, doesn't touch decorator either way).
	   This rule's own job is resetting decorator/box-shadow to neutral -- two classes here beats
	   .btn-ok's one-class declarations on specificity alone, not source order. Without it, the
	   crimson gradient/shadow would paint over the disabled neutral fill, since decorators
	   composite on top of background-color. */
	decorator: none;
	box-shadow: none;
}

/* Shared icon-only button (a monogram/glyph label instead of real art) -- consolidates what used
   to be two independent, near-identical copies in login_main.rcss and char_sel_main.rcss. Each
   consuming window keeps its own positional class (.btn-icon-menu/.btn-icon-credit,
   #btn_create/#btn_menu/etc.) for placement -- this shared class only carries the reusable
   look. */
.btn-icon {
	position: absolute;
	width: 54dp;
	height: 30dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	background-color: token(surface-control);
	border: 1dp token(border-frame);
	border-radius: token(radius-md)dp;
	text-align: center;
	line-height: 30dp;
}
.btn-icon:hover {
	background-color: token(accent-steel);
	border: 1dp token(accent-steel);
}
.btn-icon.disabled {
	pointer-events: none;
	opacity: 0.5;
	cursor: default;
	background-color: token(surface-control);
	border: 1dp token(border-frame);
}
/* font-family/size often need redeclaring directly on the label span rather than relying on
   inheritance -- see char_sel_main.rcss's/credit_win.rcss's own comments on this build's
   inheritance quirk inside a data-model-bound document. */
.btn-icon-label {
	color: token(text-primary);
	font-weight: bold;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}

/* Reusable window-layering primitives. Intended usage: apply .modern-frame to the outer
   positioned panel element itself (it keeps that element's own position/size), then add ONE
   decorative child div with .modern-panel, inset a few px on all sides, as the FIRST child (so it
   paints behind real content, same convention as .layout-anchor/.panel-header) -- the small
   reveal between the frame's inner edge and the panel's own border is deliberate, it's the
   frame's own material showing as a third visual band. Real content stays direct children of the
   .modern-frame element at their original positions -- do NOT nest content inside .modern-panel,
   its inset would shift every child's effective position.

   There's no CSS variable mechanism on this engine (confirmed), so a shared class can only carry
   *structural* properties
   (border-width, the bevel/gradient mechanism) portably; a window's own specific palette (login's
   warm bronze/crimson, say) still has to come from somewhere. The pattern that actually closes
   that gap, proven by .btn/.btn-ok above: keep .modern-frame/.modern-panel as generic iron
   defaults, and add a *named palette variant* modifier class per distinct look a window
   contributes (e.g. .modern-frame-crimson below, extracted from login.rcss's own #panel) instead
   of leaving every window to hand-roll its own copy of the whole bevel mechanism from scratch.
   Apply both classes together (class="modern-frame modern-frame-crimson"); the variant only
   overrides color/decorator properties, .modern-frame's own border-width/mechanism still applies
   underneath. Window-specific structural values (width, height, border-radius, the exact inset
   used for a "leaves room for a header above" child panel) stay the consuming window's own
   responsibility either way -- these classes were never meant to carry position/size, see the
   original usage note above. */
.modern-frame {
	background-color: rgba(17, 19, 15, 196); /* surface-panel's rgb @ 196 alpha -- can't use token(surface-panel) directly, it has no alpha channel of its own */
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(32, 34, 29, 196) 0%, rgba(17, 19, 15, 196) 45%, rgba(8, 10, 8, 196) 100%);
	border-width: 2dp;
	border-top-color: rgba(92, 96, 88, 160); /* metal-edge's rgb @ 160 alpha: bevel-light, brighter than the fill for a real edge-catch */
	border-left-color: rgba(92, 96, 88, 160);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190); /* bevel-dark */
	border-right-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190);
}
.modern-panel {
	position: absolute;
	left: 4dp;
	right: 4dp;
	top: 4dp;
	bottom: 4dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: token(surface-recessed);
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(14, 16, 13, 180) 0%, rgba(7, 8, 7, 180) 100%);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-inner); /* the recessed content-well border this token exists for */
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160); /* bevel-dark-inset: recessed relative to the frame */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(107, 96, 69, 20); /* metal-warm-edge's rgb @ 20 alpha: bevel-light-inset, a faint warm catch */
	border-right-color: rgba(107, 96, 69, 20);
	border-radius: token(radius-md)dp;
}

/* Palette variant for the "real dialog" family (login/msg_win/my_quest_info/sys_menu/
   remember_password_prompt) -- extracted from login.rcss's own #panel/.login-content, the first
   real consumer of these primitives. Apply alongside .modern-frame/.modern-panel
   (class="modern-frame modern-frame-crimson"), not instead of them. Despite the name this isn't
   crimson itself (that's accent-crimson, reserved for a primary button/banner) -- it's a marginally
   warmer, slightly darker iron than plain .modern-frame, kept under its original "-crimson" name
   for now even though the frame material itself carries no red. Title/label text and the checkbox
   stay warm/gold deliberately (general body text is neutral gray, see tokens.ini's text-primary);
   only the frame/panel material itself is iron. */
.modern-frame-crimson {
	background-color: #0e0f0c;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #191b15, #0e0f0c 30%, #080907);
	border-top-color: rgba(107, 96, 69, 90); /* metal-warm-edge's rgb @ 90 alpha: a warm iron highlight catching the top edge */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
	border-right-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 220);
}
.modern-panel-crimson {
	background-color: #0a0b09;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #111210, #0a0b09 55%, #060706);
	border-top-color: rgba(107, 96, 69, 45); /* metal-warm-edge's rgb @ 45 alpha */
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 240);
	border-left-color: #050505;
	border-right-color: #050505;
}

/* Thin accent line just inside .modern-frame -- border only, no fill, so the frame's own gradient
   still shows through it. Extracted from login.rcss's .frame-accent; apply as a
   direct child of .modern-frame, inset a few px more than .modern-panel so .modern-panel's own
   position/size stays exactly as documented above -- this is a fourth visual band, not a
   replacement for the frame/panel pair. Position/size (left/right/top/bottom/border-radius) stay
   the consuming window's own responsibility, same as .modern-frame/.modern-panel themselves. */
.modern-frame-accent {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
	border-width: 1dp;
}
.modern-frame-accent-crimson {
	border-color: rgba(70, 70, 59, 166); /* metal-mid's rgb @ 166 alpha */
	border-top-color: token(accent-steel-hover);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 200);
}

/* Raised header/footer bands -- the real structural gap the first (token-only) pass over this
   theme left behind: every dialog used to float its title text over the exact same recessed
   .modern-panel surface as its body, so "header" and "body" never actually read as two different
   surfaces the way the reference's carved-metal window does. These are overlay bands (painted
   AFTER .modern-panel, same paint-order convention as .title-glow) rather than a repositioning of
   .modern-panel itself -- that keeps every already-tuned absolute-position child (title text,
   labels, buttons) exactly where it is; only a visible raised/footer band gets drawn across the
   top/bottom of the existing surface. Apply as a direct child of .modern-frame, sized by the
   consuming window (left/right + height are that window's own header-height decision, same
   convention as .modern-panel/.modern-frame-accent above). left/right/top default to the same 4dp
   inset .modern-panel/.modern-frame-accent already use -- NOT flush with the outer .modern-frame
   edge (0/0), which would paint straight over that frame's own 2dp bevel border along the top,
   hiding it under the header's opaque fill. A window can still widen it back to 0/0 locally if it
   genuinely wants the header to reach the outer edge. */
.modern-header {
	position: absolute;
	left: 4dp;
	right: 4dp;
	top: 4dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: #171813;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #24241d 0%, #171813 45%, #0d0f0c 100%);
	border-bottom-width: 1dp;
	border-bottom-color: #000000;
}
/* Small metal stud straddling the header's own top edge, centered -- a plain round rivet (real
   classic MU window hardware) rather than the reference's rotated-diamond ornament; a circle needs
   no rotation to read correctly, and stays visually distinct from .modern-joint's diamond rivet
   used elsewhere. width/height/margin-left keep it centered regardless of the header's own width,
   no transform needed. border-radius is a fixed dp value equal to half width/height, not 50% --
   this engine's border-radius parser only accepts "length" (px/dp), not percentages (confirmed
   against StyleSheetSpecification.cpp), so a percentage radius would silently fail to parse into a
   circle. */
.modern-header-rivet {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	top: -4dp;
	width: 8dp;
	height: 8dp;
	margin-left: -4dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: #0c0d0b;
	decorator: radial-gradient(circle, #6b6045 0%, #0c0d0b 75%);
	border: 1dp #484638;
	border-radius: 4dp;
}
.modern-footer {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
	bottom: 0;
	pointer-events: none;
	border-top-width: 1dp;
	border-top-color: #24251f;
}

/* Border-only recessed bevel, no fill -- for an element with legacy-drawn content painted
   underneath it (RmlUi always composites last, so any fill here would hide that content; see
   login.rcss's .input-frame comment for a concrete example). Gives the "sunk into the panel"
   read from the border bevel alone. */
.modern-inset {
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190); /* bevel-dark-inset */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 30); /* bevel-light-inset */
	border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 30);
}
.modern-inset.focused {
	border-top-color: token(accent-steel);
	border-left-color: rgba(116, 123, 116, 170); /* accent-steel's rgb @ 170 alpha */
}

/* Reusable "fading crimson banner" -- a soft ambient radial glow meant to sit behind a window's
   title text (warm-cream core cooling to a muted crimson ring, fading to transparent before the
   edge so it reads as a glow, not a hard-edged shape). Extracted from login.rcss's own
   .header-glow, the first real consumer -- a true radial falloff since RenderInterface_SDL_GPU
   implements CompileShader/RenderShader for the gradient family. Position/size
   (left/right/top/height) stay the consuming window's own responsibility, same convention as
   .modern-frame/.modern-panel above -- apply alongside a window-specific sizing class
   (class="title-glow header-glow", say), and place it as a sibling BEFORE the title text it sits
   behind (RmlUi paints earlier siblings first). */
.title-glow {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
	decorator: radial-gradient(ellipse, rgba(230, 195, 140, 110), rgba(125, 25, 15, 130) 55%, transparent 88%);
}

/* Shared tooltip shell -- every window's own single-line discoverability tooltip
   (main_frame/my_inventory/my_quest_info/mu_helper_bar/buff_strip) applies this directly.
   .tooltip__name/-type/-rule/-stat/-bonus below are additional structured-content primitives (a
   name/type/divider/stat-rows/bonus-line item tooltip) -- not consumed by any window yet (nothing
   in this codebase renders a real item tooltip through RmlUi today), laid down as an available
   primitive for whenever one is built, so it doesn't get invented ad hoc per-window later. */
.tooltip {
	background-color: token(surface-tooltip);
	border: 1dp token(border-frame);
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	color: token(text-primary);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}
.tooltip__name {
	color: token(accent-gold-hover);
	font-weight: bold;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}
.tooltip__type {
	margin-top: 2dp;
	color: token(text-secondary);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	text-transform: uppercase;
}
.tooltip__rule {
	display: block;
	height: 1dp;
	margin: 6dp 0;
	background-color: token(border-recessed);
}
.tooltip__stat {
	display: flex;
	justify-content: space-between;
	color: token(text-secondary);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}
.tooltip__stat strong {
	color: token(text-primary);
	font-weight: bold;
}
.tooltip__bonus {
	margin-top: 6dp;
	color: token(semantic-success);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}

/* Shared item-slot primitives -- generalized from main_frame.rcss's .slot-frame/.slot-fill/
   .selected (the only real "slot"-shaped RmlUi element in this theme today; the actual equipment/
   inventory grid stays fully native, see my_inventory.rml's own comment) so any future slot-based
   UI has a ready-made look instead of reinventing one. main_frame.rcss keeps its own
   .slot-frame/.slot-fill/.selected class names (its ids are bound from C++/RML, renaming them is
   a separate, unrelated risk) but is retinted to match these exactly -- see that file's own
   comment. */
.slot {
	border: 1dp token(border-frame);
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
}
.slot--filled {
	background-color: rgba(10, 10, 10, 150); /* dark backing, matches main_frame.rcss's existing .slot-fill convention -- not surface-control, that token is for a resting icon-button's light wash, the opposite direction */
}
.slot--selected {
	border-color: token(border-selected);
	background-color: rgba(180, 138, 50, 25); /* border-selected's rgb @ 25 alpha wash */
}

.hidden {
	display: none;
}

/* Layout primitives -- identical to
   themes/legacy/base.rcss's copy; pure layout, nothing theme-specific to vary. */

.anchor-top-left {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
}
.anchor-top-right {
	position: absolute;
	right: 0;
	top: 0;
}
.anchor-bottom-left {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}
.anchor-bottom-right {
	position: absolute;
	right: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}

.center-x {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
}
.center-y {
	position: absolute;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translateY(-50%);
}
.center-both {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}

.stretch-x {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
}
.stretch-y {
	position: absolute;
	top: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}
.stretch-both {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
	top: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}

/* Invisible marker read by C++ via Element::GetAbsoluteOffset() -- see themes/legacy/base.rcss's
   identical comment for the full mechanism. */
.layout-anchor {
	position: absolute;
	visibility: hidden;
	width: 0;
	height: 0;
	pointer-events: none;
}


/* ============================================================================
   Forged-dialog construction
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   These primitives are the structural counterpart to the palette tokens above.
   They intentionally use explicit RML layers + per-side bevel borders rather
   than box-shadow. The login dialog is the first consumer.
   ============================================================================ */

/* Exterior silhouette / asymmetric perimeter. Put this as the first child of a
   positioned .modern-frame. It creates the dark outer edge that browser CSS
   would normally express with an outside pseudo-element/shadow. Transparent fill -- the
   reference's own exterior layer has NO background, only a border + inset lighting (a solid fill
   here would read as a second opaque rectangle behind the window surface instead of a thin edge).
   Highlight alphas are boosted well past the reference's literal `.11`/`.08` (28/20 out of 255) --
   a literal conversion reads as invisible rendered over a real 3D game scene, not a controlled
   browser canvas. */
.modern-shell-edge {
	position: absolute;
	left: -3dp;
	right: -3dp;
	top: -3dp;
	bottom: -3dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: transparent;
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: rgba(31, 31, 26, 242);
	box-shadow:
		inset 1dp 0 0 rgba(130, 121, 91, 110),
		inset -1dp 0 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 204),
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(151, 139, 101, 90),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 230);
}

/* Inner groove, ~4dp inward -- the counterpart to .modern-shell-edge above, same reference pairing
   (`.window::before`/`.window::after`) and same visibility-boost reasoning: a thin warm hairline
   catch plus a broad inward darkness that's what actually makes the perimeter read as recessed/
   carved into the shell. */
.modern-groove {
	position: absolute;
	left: 4dp;
	right: 4dp;
	top: 4dp;
	bottom: 4dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #24261f;
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 0 0 1dp rgba(121, 112, 83, 40),
		inset 0 0 12dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 153);
}

/* Visible-width blackened-iron rail -- the reference's `.window__header` (metal, ONE center
   rivet via .modern-joint below), the alternative to .modern-title-plate's crimson hero-banner
   (never both on the same window). left/right:8dp + top:7dp matches the reference's own
   `margin:7px 8px 0` exactly. Two-layer decorator matches the reference's own `background:
   linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(205,184,129,.05),transparent 31%), linear-gradient(180deg,
   #24241d 0%,#171813 45%,#0d0f0c 100%)` -- the first (warm, translucent) layer was missing
   before sys_menu became this primitive's first real consumer. box-shadow carries the reference's
   own header shadow (`inset 0 1px 0 rgba(235,218,169,.07), inset 0 -1px 0 #000, 0 2px 4px
   rgba(0,0,0,.52)`) directly -- the inset pair is the header's own top catch-light/bottom seat,
   the outset pair is the real cast shadow proving this rail sits ABOVE whatever's below it. */
.modern-header-rail {
	position: absolute;
	left: 8dp;
	right: 8dp;
	top: 7dp;
	height: 38dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: token(metal-dark);
	decorator:
		linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(205, 184, 129, 13), transparent 31%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, token(metal-rail), #171813 45%, token(metal-rail-dark));
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-rail);
	border-bottom-color: #171812;
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(235, 218, 169, 18),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 #000000,
		0 2dp 4dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 133);
}

/* Subtle horizontal warm accent line near the rail's own bottom edge -- the reference's
   `.window__header::after` (`left:7px;right:7px;bottom:2px;height:1px;background:
   linear-gradient(90deg,transparent,#6b6045 30%,#84754e 50%,#6b6045 70%,transparent);
   opacity:.35`). Purely decorative, painted as the rail's own last child so it sits on top of the
   rail's fill. */
.modern-header-accent {
	position: absolute;
	left: 7dp;
	right: 7dp;
	bottom: 2dp;
	height: 1dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	opacity: 0.35;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to right,
		transparent,
		token(metal-warm-edge) 30%,
		#84754e 50%,
		token(metal-warm-edge) 70%,
		transparent);
}

/* Title text for .modern-header-rail -- the reference's own `<div class="window__title">...`
   (its sibling `.window__eyebrow` category label is available below too, for a window that
   actually wants that two-line stack, but a single title line reading e.g. "System Menu" already
   says what the eyebrow "System" would have -- redundant to show both here). Vertically centered
   in the rail's 38dp height via top:50%/margin-top (half a single ~13dp title line), left:11dp
   matching the reference's own `padding:0 11px`. Not used by Login (its header IS the crimson
   hero-banner/.modern-title-plate, with the server name centered directly on the plate). */
.modern-header-text {
	position: absolute;
	left: 11dp;
	top: 50%;
	margin-top: -7dp;
	pointer-events: none;
}
/* Available for a future window that wants the reference's full eyebrow+title stack (small
   uppercase category label above the real title) -- not consumed today. */
.modern-header-eyebrow {
	font-size: 7dp;
	color: #67675f;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}
.modern-header-title {
	color: token(text-warm);
	font-family: "token(font-title)";
	font-size: 13dp;
	font-weight: 500;
	letter-spacing: 0.2dp;
	font-effect: shadow(0dp 1dp #000000);
}

/* Recessed body plane. Content belongs INSIDE this element in RML. The broad inset box-shadow
   (reference: `inset 0 0 16px rgba(0,0,0,.65), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.018)`) is what
   actually reads as "physically recessed behind the frame" -- a border-color bevel alone is too
   thin an effect for a cavity this size; real box-shadow reproduces it directly instead of
   stacking extra 1dp rectangles to fake the falloff. */
.modern-content-well {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: token(surface-recessed);
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #0b0d0b, #070907);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-inner);
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 0 16dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 166),
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 5);
}

/* A structural container for the action row, not a second boxed panel. Originally this carried its
   own fill + 4-sided bevel border, which read as a second inset panel stacked directly under the
   body -- the browser reference's own footer has no independent background at all, just the
   engraved divider immediately above the buttons (see login.rcss's .login-divider-shadow/-shine).
   Kept as a real element (still needed to structurally hold the buttons, per the "no floating
   absolute buttons" requirement) but stripped down to pure position/layout -- no fill, no border,
   no visual identity of its own. */
.modern-footer-band {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* Physical crimson title plate -- this IS the hero-banner header for whichever window uses it
   (Login among them); there is no ambient glow sibling behind it and no separate metal rail under
   it (a window either uses this OR the metal .modern-header-rail/.modern-joint pair, never both).
   Two-layer decorator matches the reference's own `background: linear-gradient(180deg,
   rgba(255,217,158,.07),transparent 34%), linear-gradient(180deg,#55150f 0%,#2e0c08 56%,#160706
   100%)` exactly -- the first (warm, translucent) layer was previously missing, leaving only the
   crimson material layer below it; without it the plate read as flat crimson instead of having a
   subtle warm sheen along its own top edge. box-shadow reproduces the reference's own plate depth
   (`inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,208,145,.09), inset 0 -1px 0 #080201, 0 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,.55)`) --
   inset pair is the plate's own top highlight/bottom seat, outset is the cast shadow proving it
   sits ON/IN whatever's behind it rather than flush with it. */
.modern-title-plate {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: token(metal-title-face);
	decorator:
		linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255, 217, 158, 18), transparent 34%),
		linear-gradient(to bottom, #55150f 0%, token(metal-title-face) 56%, token(metal-title-face-dark) 100%);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-title);
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 1dp 0 rgba(255, 208, 145, 23),
		inset 0 -1dp 0 #080201,
		0 2dp 5dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 140);
}

/* Center-rivet stud for the METAL rail (.modern-header-rail, NOT .modern-title-plate) -- unused by
   any window that uses the hero-banner/title-plate header instead (Login among them; a window
   uses one header style or the other, never both). Reference: `.window__header::before` -- a real
   13x13 square rotated 45deg (apparent bounding box ~18.4dp), matching the reference's own
   `transform:rotate(45deg)` directly now that SetTransform is confirmed reliable on this
   renderer, not the axis-aligned pixel-bar stand-in an earlier pass used before that was proven.
   Position as a child of .modern-header-rail: left:50%, margin-left half its own negative width
   for centering (this engine has no calc()/translate combined with rotate in one declaration
   verified together, so centering uses the margin-offset technique already proven throughout this
   theme rather than `transform:translateX(-50%) rotate(45deg)`), top:-8dp to straddle the rail's
   own top edge. */
.modern-joint {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	top: -8dp;
	width: 13dp;
	height: 13dp;
	margin-left: -6.5dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: token(metal-groove);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #484638;
	transform: rotate(45deg);
	box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2dp #0c0d0b;
}

/* Recessed control cavity. background is flat (no decorator) and depth comes primarily from the
   inset box-shadow now, matching the reference's own input treatment (`background:#080a08;
   border:1px solid #37372e; box-shadow:inset 0 2px 7px rgba(0,0,0,.75), inset 0 0 0 1px
   rgba(113,102,74,.05)`) -- the previous version layered a decorator gradient AND per-side bevel
   border-*-colors AND a smaller inset shadow, which was a fallback approximation stacked on top of
   itself rather than the actual reference technique now that real box-shadow is available. */
.modern-control-well {
	background-color: #080a08;
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #37372e;
	box-shadow:
		inset 0 2dp 7dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 191),
		inset 0 0 0 1dp rgba(113, 102, 74, 13);
}
